Pin the Metal shading-language version instead of inheriting it from the host.

Metal derives MSL version from the SDK the *host process* linked against when
MTLCompileOptions.languageVersion is unset. ggml is a library loaded into
whichever binary the user runs, so shader compilation depended on that binary
rather than on the machine. Under python.org's CPython 3.12 (linked against the
macOS 12.1 SDK) the embedded library fails to compile entirely --
"no matching constructor for initialization of 'threadgroup metal::half4x4[512]'"
-- and the Metal backend never initializes. Below MSL 3.1 the bf16 kernels are
additionally #undef'd while props.has_bfloat stays true.

This variant targets the per-kind library layout llama.cpp's ggml adopted in
v0.3.0 (one MTLLibrary per op-source, compiled in parallel). The older
single-library layout still vendored by whisper.cpp and stable-diffusion.cpp is
handled by ggml-metal-pin-msl-version.patch; each tree matches exactly one, and
the other self-skips.

The ladder deliberately stops below MSL 4.0, so the Metal 4 tensor headers stay
unavailable and the tensor probe keeps taking its existing unsupported path.
Enabling the MSL 4.0 tensor kernels blanks stable-diffusion output, so raising
the ceiling needs its own investigation first.

